data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing modifies the genomes of living organisms and is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- A Cas9 nuclease with a synthetic guide RNA is delivered into a cell, which cuts the genome at a desired location [1].\n- Genome editing inserts, deletes, modifies, or replaces DNA, targeting site-specific locations rather than inserting material randomly [3].\n- Programmable \"molecular scissors\" create double-strand breaks in DNA [3].\n- A news headline links CRISPR to treating a broad array of diseases [4]. A conference-style paper headline reports HIV eliminated in cell culture using CRISPR-Cas editing [5]."}

